{
    "name": "WES tertiary analysis from VCF (v2)",
    "a_galaxy_workflow": "true",
    "format-version": "0.1",
    "annotation": "Tertiary analysis of a germline whole exome sequencing (WES) VCF.\n\n1. Normalise the calls against the reference: split multiallelic records, left-align indels and drop\n   records whose REF does not match the reference (bcftools norm).\n2. Keep calls inside the exome target regions, padded by a configurable number of bases so that\n   canonical splice sites are retained, and that pass the quality expression (bcftools filter).\n3. Add ClinVar clinical significance (CLNSIG, CLNREVSTAT, CLNDN, CLNSIGCONF, ALLELEID) and the\n   population allele frequency from gnomAD (stored as INFO/gnomAD_AF) with bcftools annotate.\n4. Predict functional consequences (gene, effect, impact, HGVS c./p., LOF/NMD) with SnpEff.\n5. Prioritise candidates with a SnpSift expression. The default keeps ClinVar (likely) pathogenic\n   variants at any frequency, plus rare (gnomAD AF < 1% or absent) HIGH/MODERATE impact variants.\n6. Export tab-separated tables of all annotated variants and of the candidates, and variant statistics\n   (bcftools stats, SnpEff summary) for MultiQC.\n\nMap this workflow over a collection of VCFs to process a batch of samples.\nReference FASTA, target BED, ClinVar and gnomAD must use the same genome build and the same\nchromosome naming (e.g. \"chr1\" vs \"1\") as the VCF.\n",
    "tags": [
        "WES",
        "exome",
        "variant-annotation",
        "variant-prioritization",
        "tertiary-analysis"
    ],
    "license": "MIT",
    "release": "0.1",
    "uuid": "325a3624-2876-519f-9805-0558388e1825",
    "comments": [],
